<Base lang="en" title="Fan fiction"><article class="prose"><p class="label">Writing</p><h1>Fan fiction.</h1><p>Mostly Genshin Impact, centered on one pairing (Alhaitham × Kaveh). Someone else's story, carrying my own real thinking underneath — three complete pieces, translated in full.</p><div class="ep-layout"><div class="ep-tabs"><button class="ep-tab active" data-ep="0">01 · Summer Mirage</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="1">02 · Daylight</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="2">03 · Graduation</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="3">04 · More on AO3</button></div><div class="ep-panels"><div class="ep-panel active" data-ep="0"><p>Summary: A mirage of summer — the moment I close my eyes, you're the first thing I think of.</p><p>"Summer comes around, year after year. We will meet again."</p><p>Modern AU. Character death warning.</p><p>00</p><p>"Summer comes around, year after year. We will meet again."</p><p>01</p><p>July, Yokohama.</p><p>It had been three months since he moved here from Tokyo. Ninety-three days, to be exact. The sea wind brought the same smell every day — salty, warm, sometimes mixed with the diesel drifting up from the harbor. He never tried to remember these small, tedious details. They simply refused to leave, the way things do when you'd rather forget them and can't.</p><p>Maybe it was to escape the sameness of the job. Or maybe he just wanted a different kind of life. At twenty-eight, Alhaitham packed one suitcase and a few boxes of books and moved to this seaside city. He rented an old house by the water, repainted it, fitted it with wooden shelves, and — in a nod to a hobby that hadn't quite worn off since his student days — opened a secondhand bookstore.</p><p>He named it Kagamibumidō — "Hall of the Mirrored Text" — a phrase he'd half-remembered from a dream. No one ever asked where the name came from. He never bothered to explain.</p><p>Summer was tourist season, especially in a city where history and modernity sat this close together. Streets that were usually quiet filled up overnight, and the bookstore, unexpectedly, got its share of foot traffic. No shortage of young people who were clearly from Tokyo pushed through the door — sandals, cameras around their necks, chattering in tones no one else could quite follow. They had no interest in literature. They had plenty to say about the color of a cover, the texture of the paper.</p><p>[Never mind. No one is ever really interested in literature.]</p><p>Alhaitham stood behind the counter and watched the tourists taking selfies with cold detachment. He never expected anyone to stay for a margin note, a handwritten dedication on a flyleaf, a page gone yellow with age. He didn't much care what people thought of him either — the reclusiveness, the strangeness. He'd graduated from one of the top schools in Tokyo, landed a stable position in the civil service — the kind of path most people would call enviable. And yet he'd resigned anyway, walked out of that gray office building under everyone's shock or confusion, as quietly as if he'd never belonged there at all.</p><p>He wasn't someone who liked to explain himself. Maybe explaining was already a kind of concession, and concession never suited him.</p><p>He chose Yokohama for himself. The reason was simple enough — it put him a little closer to that person.</p><p>He looked down and adjusted the grind on the coffee mill. Behind the counter sat a small bag of beans, an old brand he'd recently dug up at an import shop near the harbor. The packaging had faded, but the aroma hadn't.</p><p>Steam curled up from the pot, carrying a scent that felt like memory — and for a moment, time seemed to run backward.</p><p>Back then, there had been someone who once ran himself ragged between convenience stores and online shops for days, just to get his hands on this same seasonal bean. That idiot had shown off his prize with such smug pride, never knowing that three years later this "limited edition" would become a permanent listing, downgraded in some cafés to "beginner's beans."</p><p>"Kind of ironic, when you think about it."</p><p>He murmured it to himself, lifted his cup, took a slow sip. The bitterness pressed down the acidity just right, leaving behind a long, mellow sweetness. He stood by the window, looking past the glass toward the beach outside. From this angle the sea shimmered faintly under the sun, waves throwing back the shimmer of heat, the air seeming to warp and ripple.</p><p>Children ran in the distance. Adults sat under parasols. Someone in a straw hat lay back on a beach chair; someone else dragged a suitcase toward a departing boat.</p><p>None of these strangers belonged to him. None of them would stay.</p><p>And the person who once ran himself ragged over a bag of beans no longer belonged to this summer, either.</p><p>The coffee ran out, a few unset drops of warmth left at the bottom of the cup. He didn't refill it right away — just quietly closed the secondhand novel he'd been paging through and set it back on the shelf.</p><p>He knew that person would never push this door open again.</p><p>02</p><p>"They say if you go down to the Yokohama shore at dusk, you can see the people who've already passed on." That's what his blond senior once said.</p><p>"They call it the summer mirage."</p><p>Alhaitham frowned. "Kaveh, there's no theoretical basis for that whatsoever."</p><p>"Oh, come on, you don't have to keep dragging out that philosophy-department logic of yours all the time — try being a little softer, the way we do in architecture!" Kaveh laughed like a kid, the corners of his eyes catching a soft glow in the sunset.</p><p>They were sitting on a bench by the Yokohama harbor, a convenience-store bento and an unopened bottle of soda at their feet. Kaveh loved the sea, even though he couldn't swim. Alhaitham had once made a comment about it: "Your love for the sea is about the same as a cat's love for a bath."</p><p>"That's not the same thing." Kaveh always argued back without a shred of hesitation. "I don't love the sea because I want to go in. I love it because I want to look at it."</p><p>"What you love is the distance."</p><p>"Easy for you to say." Kaveh bit down on his straw. "What about you? What do you love?"</p><p>"..."</p><p>"Alhaitham?"</p><p>"I don't know," he said quietly.</p><p>"You know everything. Of course you'd claim not to know this one." Kaveh sighed. "I don't believe you."</p><p>"Then you decide for me."</p><p>"Huh?"</p><p>"From now on, I'll love what you love."</p><p>The words came out light, almost careless — and yet they landed somewhere deep and soft in Kaveh's chest, like a promise. He didn't answer. He just let the corner of his mouth lift a little, pretending he hadn't heard.</p><p>It was the last night of their graduation trip. Kaveh had said he wanted to see if there really was a miracle waiting at the edge of the sea.</p><p>"Do you think people leave something behind, after they die?" he asked suddenly.</p><p>"In what form?"</p><p>"I don't know — sea wind. Old belongings. Wishes left unfinished."</p><p>"That's idealism talking."</p><p>"It's not." Kaveh shook his head, serious. "I just think there are places in this world that are meant for remembering."</p><p>Their conversations always went like this. Alhaitham was like stone — rational, unmoved. Kaveh was like water, always looking for the crack to seep through. They argued, they fell silent, but they always walked on side by side.</p><p>The wind that day was unusually gentle, soft enough to make you want to close your eyes and drift off.</p><p>"Are you falling asleep?" Kaveh asked quietly.</p><p>"No."</p><p>"Then I'll keep talking."</p><p>"Mm."</p><p>"When I was little, I lived far from the sea. Every time I heard the sound of waves, it was through a television."</p><p>"So that's why you always longed for it."</p><p>"Mm. But the first time I actually saw the sea was on a school trip to Yokohama." Kaveh smiled. "I snuck out of the hotel by myself to watch the sunrise on the beach. Nearly got caught by a teacher."</p><p>"You really are..."</p><p>"Reckless?"</p><p>"I was going to say — undisciplined."</p><p>"So you're a person who likes rules?"</p><p>"I like order."</p><p>"Then why are you with me now?"</p><p>Alhaitham paused, then answered: "Because you're the only person who can break my order without making me want to push you away."</p><p>Later, back at the hotel, Kaveh ignored his own exhaustion and opened his laptop to keep working on a drawing — he wanted, he said, to leave something real behind from this trip. It was the rough outline of a library, open in its structure, a skylight in the roof, like a channel where light could move freely.</p><p>"I want to build a space by this sea that can lead all the way into someone's heart."</p><p>"Are you sure it's a library, and not a temple?"</p><p>"A library is already a temple of knowledge."</p><p>"Then who's the priest?"</p><p>Kaveh tilted his head to look at him. "You are, obviously."</p><p>"I don't believe in gods."</p><p>"But you believe in me." Kaveh smiled, gentle. "That's enough."</p><p>Alhaitham said nothing. He looked down at the drawing, the ink lines still wet, holding some kind of living warmth. He reached out, suddenly, folded the drawing carefully, and slipped it into his own bag.</p><p>Kaveh blinked. "What are you doing?"</p><p>"Keeping it. Might be useful someday."</p><p>"Once it's built, we'll meet again, on this very beach."</p><p>Three years later, one summer, Alhaitham stood on that same stretch of sand, remembering that conversation, a heat rising in his chest that felt almost like pain.</p><p>The sea wind was still salty and damp. But Kaveh was long gone.</p><p>He closed his eyes, trying to trace that person's shape out of the shifting wind.</p><p>He even tried to find that drawing again. He never could. Maybe it was lost in a move. Maybe it got thrown out by accident during some cleanup.</p><p>But he still remembered exactly what that library looked like. He remembered the wide skylight in the roof. He remembered Kaveh once saying, "I want this place to be somewhere you can sit all day and feel at peace, even if your heart is full of holes."</p><p>A mirage is, in the end, only a mirage.</p><p>— And real longing is a weight that even the wind cannot imitate.</p><p>"Summer comes around, year after year. We will meet again."</p><p>He remembers, with perfect clarity, that this was the last thing Kaveh ever said to him that summer.</p><p>03</p><p>The day Kaveh died, the weather was unnaturally clear.</p><p>Clear to the point of absurdity. Clear like it was mocking something.</p><p>Not a single cloud over Tokyo. The buildings stood sharp-edged against the light, throwing back a brightness that felt wrong for the hour. Alhaitham had just come up from the subway, on his way to the café where he and Kaveh always met — a small independent shop on a street corner, run by one of Kaveh's old clients, an old architect with a taste for the retro. They always sat there for at least two hours whenever they met. Kaveh drank hot lemon water. He drank black coffee.</p><p>The text arrived at 12:43.</p><p>"Hello, are you a family member or emergency contact for Mr. Kaveh?"</p><p>For a moment, time seemed to hit pause. The noise around him faded. Even the sunlight didn't feel hot anymore.</p><p>It was a message from the construction site.</p><p>The project Kaveh was overseeing was in Yamanashi — a renovation of a school complex in the mountains. They'd been on the phone the night before; Kaveh had said the weather was ridiculously good, that they might finish early tomorrow and he'd swing down the mountain to buy some peaches on the way back.</p><p>"Didn't you tell me not to eat random things all the time?"</p><p>"You're up in the mountains now. Not my problem anymore."</p><p>"Ha — see, you really do worry about me."</p><p>"...Get back soon."</p><p>"Wait for me. I'll bring you peaches."</p><p>But there was never going to be a "tomorrow."</p><p>The accident happened suddenly. A steel frame collapsed without warning. A few workers were lightly injured. Kaveh, who'd been explaining a structural drawing to a new intern, happened to be standing directly beneath where it fell.</p><p>"He shielded the kid."</p><p>"If he hadn't turned back, he might have gotten clear."</p><p>These were the words Alhaitham heard a few days later, at the funeral.</p><p>Kaveh's coworkers stood in front of him, eyes red, repeating the same lines over and over. They said Kaveh was a hero. They said he reacted fast. They said he didn't even have time to be afraid in that last moment — but none of those words landed gently. Every one of them drove into Alhaitham's mind like a nail.</p><p>"He's dead."</p><p>Alhaitham kept telling himself this fact, over and over. He'd read countless philosophical texts, debated "existence" and "nothingness," "consciousness" and "death" — but when it actually happened to someone beside him, all that knowledge turned out to be worthless paper.</p><p>He doesn't even remember how he got through those days.</p><p>Yamanashi to Tokyo, back to Yamanashi again, arranging the transport of the body, cataloguing his belongings, signing off on the funeral arrangements. He turned away every relative's condolences and let only one of Kaveh's old college friends stay to help him handle it all.</p><p>That friend handed him an old suitcase, said Kaveh had asked him to keep it, just in case.</p><p>"He said — if anything ever happened to him, give it to you."</p><p>The case wasn't large. Inside were scattered drawings, drafting tools, and a notebook with a worn cover.</p><p>The first page read:</p><p>"If one day I can't finish this building with my own hands, please draw the last line for me."</p><p>Signed with Kaveh's name, in that same messy but unwavering signature.</p><p>Alhaitham closed the notebook and sat in silence for a long time.</p><p>Kaveh's funeral was simple. No religious rites, no drawn-out mourning ceremony. He'd said, while alive, that he didn't like that kind of formality.</p><p>"What do you want, after you die?" he'd once asked Alhaitham, out of nowhere, late one night.</p><p>"To not trouble anyone, as much as possible."</p><p>"Figures." Kaveh sighed. "But if I die, you have to come see me off."</p><p>"Why would I do that?"</p><p>"You're so cold, you might not even come collect my ashes."</p><p>"You worry too much. I'd collect them."</p><p>"And then what?"</p><p>"Then... put them in your library."</p><p>"So you finally admit it's my library?"</p><p>"You drew it. I just did the labor."</p><p>Kaveh had laughed like a kid.</p><p>But now he was really gone.</p><p>What he left behind wasn't just a stack of drawings and a heap of memories, but a great many blank spaces meant for the future — the places they'd promised to go, the films they'd never finished watching, the arguments left half-argued.</p><p>Three days after the funeral, Alhaitham went back to the apartment they'd once shared. He sat alone at the desk, paging through that notebook.</p><p>Tucked inside was a yellowed sticky note.</p><p>"If you're reading this, it means I really was careless."</p><p>"But I think you'll keep going."</p><p>"Don't lock yourself in the dark."</p><p>"Even a mirage is worth the walk."</p><p>— Kaveh</p><p>He didn't sleep that night.</p><p>Outside, the cicadas of July droned on, the air thick and heavy like a rain that refused to fall.</p><p>He sat there, looking at the drawing.</p><p>What Kaveh had designed was a library by the sea. The lines were open, unhurried; the facade let the light through; there was a wind corridor, a reading area, a terrace.</p><p>Alhaitham's finger traced slowly along that wind corridor, and finally stopped over a patch of blacked-out space.</p><p>Written on it were the words:</p><p>"The space left for you."</p><p>The next day, Alhaitham took an extended leave, packed his things, and — with that suitcase and a stack of drawings — set off for Yokohama.</p><p>He returned to the harbor town they'd once passed through together, and rented that old house by the sea.</p><p>He renovated it, bought the books, built the shelves. He wasn't an architect, but Kaveh's drawings were detailed enough that they'd accounted for the load-bearing walls, the direction of the light, everything.</p><p>Kagamibumidō slowly began to take shape.</p><p>Deep at night, he'd sit by the window listening to the sea, Kaveh's notebook resting near his hand. Sometimes he'd talk to himself. Sometimes he'd say nothing at all, just stare at the book, lost in thought.</p><p>"Didn't you say there'd be a miracle here?" he asked, once.</p><p>No one answered.</p><p>The wind tapped gently against the window, like a dream that had traveled a long way and, in the end, never knocked on the door.</p><p>But Alhaitham knew — even a mirage deserves someone to keep a light burning for it.</p><p>So he lit that lamp, for everyone who had lost someone they loved in the middle of a summer.</p><p>And for himself, too.</p><p>04</p><p>They had once lived together in a small south-facing apartment, fourth floor, no elevator, close to the university's back gate. Climbing the stairs always left them a little winded, but Kaveh insisted on staying there, saying it had "good sunlight, gentle wind, and it's quiet enough."</p><p>"An architect who's really just a professional apartment-picker," Alhaitham had said, the first day he moved in.</p><p>Kaveh hadn't argued. He'd just raised an eyebrow. "You can sleep at night. That's all because of the window orientation I chose."</p><p>The apartment wasn't big. Past the entryway was a narrow living room; the kitchen only fit one person turning around at a time; the desk sat right against the balcony, where two potted plants stood — a eucalyptus and a small daisy. Kaveh had brought them home one day from the flower market, saying, "A home should have something living in it."</p><p>"Am I not living?" Alhaitham asked.</p><p>"You're the kind of living thing that doesn't express its feelings well."</p><p>"You're saying I'm like a cactus."</p><p>"If you had spines, I'd have been stabbed to death by now."</p><p>—</p><p>Their life together was simple.</p><p>Kaveh always made sandwiches for breakfast, frying eggs while humming some off-key tune — sometimes an Arabic folk song, sometimes some jumble he'd picked up at an architecture-department dinner. Alhaitham usually sat at the table reading, a cup of unsweetened black coffee beside him.</p><p>"Eat some fruit," Kaveh would remind him often.</p><p>"I take supplements for the vitamins."</p><p>"Humans don't survive on pills."</p><p>"I survive on coffee."</p><p>"You'll wreck your nerves eventually."</p><p>"Aren't you the one running on sugar?"</p><p>"I run on love." Kaveh cracked himself up saying it.</p><p>In the afternoons they'd sometimes go to the library together. Alhaitham read philosophy, linguistics, logic, in the original languages; Kaveh always sat in front of the architecture section, sketching.</p><p>"That's not architecture you're drawing," Alhaitham once pointed out, tapping his notebook.</p><p>"That's a feeling."</p><p>"You draw with feeling. I think with feeling."</p><p>"Don't mock me." Kaveh snapped the notebook shut. "You always do this."</p><p>"Don't you think you're too romantic?"</p><p>"Don't you think you're too cold?"</p><p>They argued often, but never for more than half an hour. Kaveh was quick to temper, and mid-argument he'd often just grab him in a hug: "Fine, I won't fight with you anymore. Just kiss me and we're done."</p><p>Alhaitham always gave in, reluctantly: "You use that trick every single time."</p><p>"Only because it works on you every single time."</p><p>—</p><p>Late at night, they'd often sit side by side on the balcony, listening to the sound of someone unloading stock at the convenience store down below, the city's breathing drifting up slowly from the dark.</p><p>"Do you think, when we're old, we'll still be sitting here like this?"</p><p>"If you don't move out."</p><p>"Will you always live with me?"</p><p>"As long as you don't smash cups every time we fight."</p><p>"So you'll put up with me?"</p><p>"You're worth it."</p><p>"What if I disappear one day?"</p><p>"If you dare, I'll drag you back from the underworld myself."</p><p>Kaveh went quiet for a moment, then leaned against his shoulder and murmured, "Don't wear yourself out."</p><p>—</p><p>And then Kaveh really did disappear.</p><p>After that, Alhaitham moved out of that apartment and went to Yokohama, opened Kagamibumidō. Inside the bookstore he recreated the old balcony arrangement — a eucalyptus, a small daisy, and two chairs.</p><p>But he knew no one would ever sit in the other one again.</p><p>He kept the old habits. Black coffee in place of breakfast, though sometimes he'd still fry an egg sandwich — just with no one left to nag him about the fruit. At night he'd sit by the window watching the sea, and whenever the wind blew he'd turn his head on instinct, half-expecting to see Kaveh come out in his pajamas, rubbing his eyes: "Why aren't you asleep yet?"</p><p>Memory has weight.</p><p>It sits, heavy, in every ordinary motion — the gesture of brewing coffee, the pause of a finger between the pages of a book. Alhaitham understood that this weight was something he would never, for the rest of his life, be able to set down.</p><p>Once he dreamed Kaveh was standing on the old balcony, smiling at him.</p><p>"I'm still waiting for you," Kaveh said.</p><p>"Waiting for what?"</p><p>"For you to finish living this life, and then come back and yell at me for eating whatever I want."</p><p>He laughed, in the dream. But when he woke, the corners of his eyes were wet.</p><p>Outside the window, another day of July.</p><p>He walked into the bookstore and stopped in front of Kaveh's little daisy.</p><p>"The wind's gentle today," he said. "Good weather for drawing."</p><p>He sat down and opened the old notebook, continuing to trace out the drawings Kaveh had left behind.</p><p>His lines had always been cold and precise, but on this page, they seemed to carry a little extra, unhurried softness.</p><p>"If you were still here, you'd draw it better."</p><p>The curtain stirred in the wind, like some silent answer.</p><p>He looked up, and saw the sea outside the window, bright as a dream that hadn't ended yet.</p><p>05</p><p>Yokohama, July. The sun was gradually leaning west.</p><p>The wind came in off the harbor, carrying the smell of salt and sand, as if every inch of air held a memory folded inside it. Alhaitham stood at the door of Kagamibumidō, a freshly brewed coffee still in hand.</p><p>For once, he hadn't made it black — he'd brewed a latte with milk instead, the way Kaveh always liked it.</p><p>The store was quiet.</p><p>The afternoon crowd had already thinned out; the wind chime by the door rang out now and then, a bright, lonely sound. He leaned against the counter, his gaze drifting to the empty chair across from him.</p><p>That chair had never once been moved since the store opened. No one had ever really sat in it.</p><p>But he always felt it wasn't empty.</p><p>—</p><p>That summer, he and Kaveh had come to Yokohama too.</p><p>Backpacks on, flip-flops soaked through, running along the beach until they were both drenched in sweat. Kaveh had insisted on riding the Ferris wheel by the shore, saying he wanted to photograph "what the sea wind looks like on your face."</p><p>"You took fifty pictures of me — which one turned out good?" Alhaitham had asked.</p><p>Kaveh flipped through the camera. "The second one. You're smiling in it."</p><p>"That wasn't a smile. That was the wind blowing in my eyes."</p><p>"You smiled. That's a smile."</p><p>Now he'd had that photo printed and framed behind the counter.</p><p>In it, he's turned slightly to the side, his hair mussed by the wind, something a little tired in his eyes — but there's a faint, unmistakable curve at the corner of his mouth. That was the moment Kaveh pressed the shutter.</p><p>"This angle catches the light best," Kaveh had said, back then.</p><p>—</p><p>Toward evening, a white-haired old man walked into the store, asking if there were any old books on Yokohama folklore.</p><p>Alhaitham led him to the shelf in the corner.</p><p>"Have you heard of the 'summer mirage'?" the old man asked suddenly, flipping through a book.</p><p>"I have," he answered flatly.</p><p>"I actually saw it once, when I was young." The old man smiled. "At dusk, on the beach — a figure standing between the tideline and the sand, just looking at me, perfectly still. By the time I ran over, there was no one there."</p><p>"Did you recognize him?"</p><p>"My older brother. He passed away many years ago."</p><p>Alhaitham went still for a moment.</p><p>The old man went on: "It wasn't a ghost, and it wasn't a hallucination. I knew it was him. He came to see whether I'd lived well."</p><p>"Did you ever see him again after that?"</p><p>"No." The old man shook his head. "He knew I was living. So he left."</p><p>After the old man left, Alhaitham sat down in that chair.</p><p>He suddenly thought of something Kaveh had once said — "Even a mirage is worth the walk."</p><p>He stood, closed the store, took his sketchbook and pencil, and walked out toward the beach.</p><p>—</p><p>The sun was just setting.</p><p>The sea had turned a burning orange, light scattered across the water like broken gold. There weren't many people on the sand — the occasional couple walking hand in hand, a child chasing after the waves. Alhaitham crossed the shallows and kept walking, all the way to where the harbor met the sea.</p><p>The sea wind hit his face, damp and gentle.</p><p>He stopped, and slowly closed his eyes.</p><p>Somewhere in the wind, someone seemed to whisper, softly, in his ear —</p><p>"You finally came."</p><p>His eyes snapped open.</p><p>Far off, on the line where the sea met the shore, a figure stood.</p><p>Blond hair lifting lightly in the wind, an outline too familiar to mistake.</p><p>Kaveh.</p><p>He stood on sand still wet with surf, wearing that white shirt that had disappeared long ago, the corner of his mouth curved in a smile — as if he'd stepped out of some parallel world, just to see him one more time.</p><p>Alhaitham said nothing. He simply walked toward him, step by step.</p><p>With every step, the memories surged back —</p><p>Kaveh drinking hot lemon water. Kaveh shattering a glass cup. Kaveh's murmured, sleepless words in his arms in the middle of the night. The last note Kaveh ever left him.</p><p>The water rose slowly past his ankles. The figure hadn't faded yet.</p><p>"Are you here?" he finally spoke.</p><p>Kaveh nodded.</p><p>"Are you... still angry with me?"</p><p>Kaveh shook his head.</p><p>"Why didn't you get out of the way?"</p><p>Kaveh smiled. "Because I saw the text you sent that day."</p><p>"I never sent it."</p><p>"But you thought about it."</p><p>"You knew?"</p><p>"Of course. You're the person I know best."</p><p>He reached out a hand, and touched nothing.</p><p>Kaveh's outline began to blur as the sun sank, like a pencil drawing slowly wiped away by the sea wind.</p><p>"Don't go," he said quietly.</p><p>"I'm not going anywhere," Kaveh answered softly. "You're just the one who has to keep going now."</p><p>"How am I supposed to keep going alone?"</p><p>"You're not alone."</p><p>"I don't have you anymore."</p><p>"You have me." Kaveh's voice was growing distant now. "In every place you remember. In every place you've drawn me. In every cup of coffee you drink. In every lamp you light at night."</p><p>"I miss you."</p><p>"I miss you too."</p><p>The last light sank into the sea, and the world fell still.</p><p>He stood where he was, letting the tide rise past his ankles, past his knees, until the wind gently smoothed the water flat again.</p><p>The beach was empty now, except for him — even his footprints washed away by the sea.</p><p>He went back to the store and lit a lamp in front of the little daisy.</p><p>The lamp was old, something they'd found together at a flea market once. Kaveh had always said it was ugly, but he'd never once been willing to replace it.</p><p>The warm yellow light fell across the drawing in front of him, and lit up the photograph on the top shelf.</p><p>He sat down and opened the notebook.</p><p>On that page of blank space he'd left untouched for so long, he finally drew the last line.</p><p>The curve was clean, the lines rounded — exactly the way Kaveh used to draw.</p><p>"See," he said quietly, "now it's complete."</p><p>Outside, the wind stirred the chime again, like someone answering.</p><p>The summer mirage came to a close.</p><p>But that lamp — it would go on burning.</p></div>
<div class="ep-panel" data-ep="1"><p>You are what you love</p><p>— epigraph</p><p>00</p><p>"What? You're going to the Desert of Hadramaveth?" Tighnari's ears twitched — a bigger reaction than usual for the fennec fox, like he'd just heard something outrageous. "I didn't think you'd go that far just chasing inspiration."</p><p>"You'll get used to it, Tighnari. Scholars are all a little insane, you know that. And didn't you once check yourself into a health center just to tell poisonous mushrooms apart from safe ones? More than once, actually." Cyno calmly flicked out four ice dice, and Kamisato Ayaka's ultimate promptly demolished the opponent's Ganyu.</p><p>"But..."</p><p>"I'm just saying, Cyno, could you go easy on me a little? This is the eighth time I've lost a card game to you today. Ugh, forget it, I'm done." Kaveh waved a hand and gloomily gathered up his deck, handing it back to Tighnari.</p><p>"No. You know I take Genius Invokation more seriously than my own job. So what disease has you running off to some place that far away this time?" Cyno tucked the deck back into his own pouch, arms crossed, uncharacteristically skipping his usual dad jokes. He looked at Kaveh, serious. "You should know — outside of the Eremites who once left the Akademiya, basically no one has set foot there in a few hundred years."</p><p>"I'd second what Cyno's saying. The Grand Sage knows everything about the desert — a place no one's touched in centuries is genuinely dangerous." Tighnari spread the cards Kaveh had handed him back out. "Sigh, and I finally built a decent deck too. Didn't expect the guy holding it to be this bad at the game."</p><p>"Hey! You two, is it fun ganging up on someone who barely even plays this game?" Kaveh instantly puffed up like an Anemo Slime. "And besides, it's not like I woke up one day and decided to go to Hadramaveth on a whim."</p><p>Tighnari looked at him, half-teasing. "So why, then? Fought with Alhaitham again? Running away from home?"</p><p>"As if I'd want to fight with that guy!"</p><p>Cyno and Tighnari exchanged a look, both quietly confirming: yep, he'd fought with Alhaitham.</p><p>"I'm telling you, both of you! I... I have nothing to say to that guy anyway! Besides, the reason I've decided to go to Hadramaveth this time is because I heard from the Traveler that the gate to Khaenri'ah is buried underground somewhere in that region — this is a perfect chance to study Khaenri'ahn architecture." Kaveh looked thoroughly pleased with himself. "Once I study their designs and rework them into my own style, I'll definitely land more commissions, make a decent chunk of money, and then I can finally move out of Alhaitham's place."</p><p>"But have you considered — because of the catastrophe five hundred years ago, the underground of Khaenri'ah is probably crawling with monsters. If something happens to you..." Before Tighnari could finish, Kaveh cut him off. "It's fine, I'm a Vision holder, and Mehrak is really capable! Besides, I've been through this kind of thing plenty of times, I'm used to it."</p><p>Kaveh grabbed the cloak draped over the back of his chair. "Alright, it's getting late, I should go pack for the trip." Then he suddenly seemed to remember something and spun back around to face Cyno and Tighnari: "And remember — do not, under any circumstances, tell Alhaitham I'm going to Hadramaveth. I really don't want to run into that annoying guy while I'm doing fieldwork."</p><p>The door clicked shut behind him, and after a short silence, Cyno spoke first. "Tighnari, want to play another round?"</p><p>"Sure, sure. Honestly I don't know what to say about Kaveh anymore."</p><p>"It's fine. Alhaitham will handle him."</p><p>01</p><p>"So what big project has our great architect latched onto this time?" Alhaitham leaned in the doorway, arms crossed, watching Kaveh scramble to pack, his tone flat, without a ripple.</p><p>"That's my business. Do I need to report it to you?" Catching sight of that expressionless face out of the corner of his eye, Kaveh suddenly remembered yesterday's business with Alhaitham swiping his key again. Taking the key was one thing — the worse part was how insincere Alhaitham's apology had been afterward, his whole face basically saying: yes, I was wrong, and what exactly are you going to do about it. The memory alone was enough to make Kaveh's temper flare instantly. He shoved his drafting tools haphazardly into Mehrak, brushed past Alhaitham without another word, and marched toward the entryway.</p><p>Watching his roommate's sulking profile, Alhaitham — sharp as ever — immediately understood he was still angry about the key incident yesterday. He'd meant to make peace with the osmanthus wine he'd hidden away in the cabinet, freshly arrived from Liyue, but by the time the door slammed shut behind Kaveh, Alhaitham could only shake his head, a little helpless. He set the book in his hand back on the shelf and happened to notice the key Kaveh had left behind on the coffee table — a little brass lion, grinning, which made him think, again, of that familiar face. Whatever. Let him go, Alhaitham thought, and casually slipped the key into his own belt pouch, then tucked the osmanthus wine into the fridge on his way past.</p><p>Kaveh never expected that one offhand comment from the Traveler would end up costing him five days on the road. He wasn't unfamiliar with the desert, but his previous travels had never gone further than Aaru Village and the ruins near the Tanit Camp. Even as a Vision holder with plenty of desert-exploration experience under his belt, the rugged, ever-shifting terrain of the hill country still cost even Kaveh a great deal of effort before he finally set foot in Mt. Damāvand.</p><p>The moment he laid eyes on the landscape in front of him, "stunning" no longer felt like a strong enough word. The scenery was nothing like any desert he'd seen before — desolate, but carrying a kind of tragic, epic weight. According to the Traveler, before this land was purified, the black sludge connecting to the sky seemed to signal that doomsday could arrive at any moment; because so few people had set foot here over the centuries, the whole place carried an air of eerie desolation.</p><p>What he was looking at now was the landscape after the Traveler's purification — still somewhat bleak, but that streak of green made Kaveh feel like this lifeless place finally had a bit of life in it after all.</p><p>Kaveh pulled out the hand-drawn map the Traveler had given him from his toolbox. Following it, he made his way smoothly into the underground Ruin Serpent's — the copper-and-jasper city hidden below. Kaveh had known a little about Khaenri'ah before this, but what he knew was limited to the fact that it was once a technologically advanced underground nation, the very kingdom whose ruin sparked the catastrophe that swept across Teyvat five hundred years ago — beyond that, all he really knew was that two enormous ruin guardians stood watch at the edges of Sumeru's rainforest and desert. Kaveh scratched his head; even having braced himself, the sheer scale of the workshop before him and the tangled, winding terrain still made him suck in a sharp breath.</p><p>"Man, I really should've asked the Traveler more before coming — this place is even creepier than I expected." Grumbling at his own oversight, Kaveh pressed the control panel for the elevator platform. It descended slowly, and once he reached the ground level, following the map's guidance, he defeated a Hilichurl Rogue, scrambled over a pile of loose rock, and finally saw the gate to Khaenri'ah that the Traveler had described to him.</p><p>02</p><p>The once-glorious nation no longer existed — all that remained was this shut gate and the ruin spreading out around it. A black sun emblem, unique to Khaenri'ah, was carved into the door, and a single wilting lily bloomed near the entrance, as if silently narrating the disaster and the cries this nation had once endured. The silence around him was almost frightening — no one else was nearby, and yet Kaveh could hear, clearly, an unbroken chain of screaming and sobbing. The noise turned his mind to mush. He shook his head hard, several times, trying to shake the sound loose. Ley line disorder — that was the first thought that surfaced in his mind.</p><p>Finally, a little quiet, Kaveh thought. This place really was as dangerous and unstable as Cyno and Tighnari had warned. Kaveh remembered a Rtawahist scholar once mentioning that ley line disorder erodes a person's nerves and drives them mad — even a Vision holder wasn't immune. Kaveh forced himself to take two deep breaths, and once he felt his heartbeat steady again, he found a new spot to sit, brought out Mehrak, and pulled out his sketchbook.</p><p>His pencil moved back and forth across the page, and within a few strokes Kaveh had already blocked out the rough shape of the Khaenri'ah gate. Just as he stepped forward to get a closer look at the details of the black sun emblem, the familiar headache returned. Kaveh backed away from the gate at once, both hands pressed to his head, trying again to shake the pain loose.</p><p>"Phew, phew, that should do it."</p><p>Kaveh tried approaching the mysterious gate again, but this time he noticed an inconspicuous stone block in a corner near the door that didn't match the pattern of the wall. Every other stone was laid in neat vertical rows — except this one, which was laid horizontal. Kaveh tilted his head; from his time exploring the Ruins of Kishtwar, he'd run into all sorts of mechanisms before. Could this out-of-place stone be one too?</p><p>Curious, he gave it a push. The stone slid in easily, and as the wall behind it slowly retreated, a hidden chamber revealed itself before him. The chamber seemed to be a long spiral staircase leading upward. Kaveh forced himself to take another deep breath, then stepped inside.</p><p>The moment he crossed the threshold, the stone braziers around him lit up on their own. Kaveh had to admit — no wonder Khaenri'ah had been the pinnacle of technology; even its mechanisms were more advanced than the heat-sensor traps at the Ruins of Kishtwar. Following the stairs, Kaveh finally reached the end of the passage, and thanks to the firelight, he could make out what lay ahead clearly.</p><p>It was a fairly large stone chamber; the walls were somewhat crumbled, but the room as a whole had been remarkably well preserved. Kaveh looked up at the ceiling — yes, exactly as described in the books, a classic Khaenri'ahn coffered ceiling. Most of it was intact, but one section had caved in, and through that broken gap Kaveh could clearly see the sky outside — pitch black, telling him it was already deep night.</p><p>Kaveh kept walking deeper into the chamber, a little discouraged; if it was already night outside, that meant he'd been underground for a very long time — long enough that he'd lost track of it entirely. Finally, he reached the far end of the chamber, and what greeted him was a statue. Two figures, arms around each other, locked in a passionate kiss. Kaveh froze for a second — in Sumeru, where art was so strictly regulated, coming across a piece this exquisite was a genuinely rare thing for an artist like him. He studied the sculpture carefully, and it was only then that he noticed what set it apart: the figure with the handsome profile and the beautiful head of curls was male — meaning both figures locked in this kiss were men, not the conventional man-and-woman pairing. The dizziness and nausea from the ley line disorder hit him again, and Kaveh couldn't stop himself from retching in pain, one hand pressed to his chest to steady his breathing, crouching down where he stood to try to ease the discomfort. After a while, once the nausea seemed to have passed, Kaveh slowly stood back up and kept staring, transfixed, at the statue in front of him.</p><p>While he was lost in thought, he noticed Mehrak, without waiting for any instruction, drift on its own toward a small mound of earth beside the statue. Noticing Mehrak's behavior, Kaveh felt a spark of excitement — Mehrak was something he'd built by modifying an ancient core, so if it was acting like this, it must have found something significant. He stepped forward quickly, dug into the mound, and uncovered a slab of solid black stone, densely carved with text.</p><p>"This is the treasure you found?"</p><p>The expression on Mehrak's face made it clear it had full confidence in its own discovery.</p><p>"Come on, how is a broken slab of stone supposed to count as treasure?" Even as he grumbled about it, Kaveh still picked up the slab and turned it over carefully in his hands.</p><p>Just as Kaveh was absorbed in studying the tablet, he caught a blurred, grayish-green shape out of the corner of his eye. In the orange firelight, that figure's outline slowly sharpened.</p><p>03</p><p>"Alhaitham? What are you doing here?"</p><p>"Why shouldn't I be here?"</p><p>"Hmph, I bet you snuck off work to come slack off here, didn't you?"</p><p>"Not quite. I'm actually here on official business, this time. Besides..." Alhaitham casually pulled out the key with the little lion keychain from his belt pouch and dangled it in front of Kaveh on purpose. "I was worried a certain someone might not have noticed he left without his key."</p><p>"You — you—" Kaveh's temper flared instantly. "That's just low, Alhaitham!"</p><p>"Let's be clear, someone forgot to take it himself. I was just being nice enough to point it out." Alhaitham opened the book in his hand. "And like I said, I'm here on official business."</p><p>"So exactly what kind of official business drags our great Scribe all the way out to this godforsaken place?"</p><p>"As you can see, the Haravatat is dealing with a thorny research problem lately, so they sent me to study the ancient Khaenri'ahn script."</p><p>"Never seen you work overtime in the middle of the night before — a rare sight. Guess even our great Scribe has days he's led around by the nose."</p><p>Alhaitham glanced at Kaveh. "That slab in your hands happens to be written in Khaenri'ahn script."</p><p>"Ah, no wonder I couldn't read it. Well, since our great Scribe is here to study the writing anyway, I suppose translating the text on the slab is no trouble for you either?" Kaveh tossed the slab to him, annoyed, then sat down with his back to him, facing the statue, pulling out his sketchbook. "Just so we're clear, you translate — don't bother me while I'm drawing."</p><p>Alhaitham caught the slab and sat down with his back to Kaveh's. He worked through the text against the reference book in hand, but every so often, he'd tilt his head just enough to catch Kaveh out of the corner of his eye.</p><p>The young man sat hunched over his sketchbook, his blond hair falling just enough to shadow that fine profile, only his red eyes catching a faint flicker of excitement. The soft scratch of pencil against paper made Alhaitham's heartbeat skip, just for a moment.</p><p>Watching Kaveh so absorbed in his drawing, Alhaitham couldn't deny it — he was selfish. For a long time now he'd carried some inexplicable possessiveness toward Kaveh; when Kaveh was happy, he was happy too, and though he kept it off his face, he couldn't help worrying whenever Kaveh was upset. Maybe, somewhere in his mind, Kaveh had already become something set apart from everyone else. He had to admit — as much as he'd always been someone who put himself first — Kaveh's importance to him had long since outgrown his own. When, exactly, had Kaveh come to matter this much? Why was it that every time something involving Kaveh came up, the rationality he took such pride in simply vanished?</p><p>He suddenly remembered the first time he'd ever seen Kaveh. It was a mild, sunlit afternoon; he'd come to the House of Daena on his advisor's orders to look for research material. The Akademiya was quiet on Sundays, with few students around, and Alhaitham — someone who disliked being bothered — had always enjoyed that kind of undisturbed atmosphere. As he wove through the bookshelves searching for material, he found Kaveh, asleep face-down on a desk, worn out from staying up all night working on a drafting assignment.</p><p>The mellow afternoon light drifted lazily in through the ornate stained-glass windows, falling across the young man's shoulders. Beside his hand lay a half-finished drafting sheet; his chest rose and fell steadily with his breathing, showing no sign of waking anytime soon. The sunlight stretched the blond youth's shadow long across the pristine white floor of the House of Daena, the window's floral pattern reflected beside it, beautiful as an oil painting with the boy at its center — one he had no idea he was starring in.</p><p>Alhaitham stood there, arms crossed, quietly taking in the scene. The young man's handsome profile struck him as impressive, and at the same time faintly amusing — who does their homework only halfway and then just falls asleep face-down on the table? But it occurred to him quickly enough that bright, warm weather was, after all, perfect for napping, and that the sleeping stranger's behavior was simply human nature.</p><p>Alhaitham never quite noticed how long he'd been quietly watching that young man. After a while, a girl hurried over toward the desk where he was napping. Something seemed to have her flustered; the white academy emblem pinned to her hat marked her as a Rtawahist. Alhaitham thought to himself. The girl rushed toward the sleeping young man, but he was sleeping too soundly to notice anyone approaching. Left with no choice, she tapped his shoulder, and the young man jolted awake — though the groggy look on his face made it clear his brain hadn't fully rebooted yet.</p><p>"Senior Kaveh! Our project is due tomorrow, how's your draft coming along?"</p><p>Those ruby-red eyes blinked, and the young man froze for a second before he caught sight of the half-finished drafting sheet under his hand, snapped back to reality, and pressed his palms together apologetically toward the girl. "Sorry, sorry, I'll definitely finish it tonight."</p><p>The girl pursed her lips, unhappy. "You'd better hurry, then. Our last group meeting is at nine tonight. I came all this way specifically to remind you, because you're always the one who's late to meetings, Senior Kaveh."</p><p>Kaveh scratched his head, embarrassed, and swore up and down to the girl that he wouldn't be late this time. The girl smiled, as if she'd won some kind of victory.</p><p>What an interesting person, Alhaitham thought. He put the book in his hand back on the shelf and pulled down a different file.</p><p>After that day, he learned that the young man with the blond hair and red eyes was named Kaveh.</p><p>After that — whether by coincidence or not — he noticed that every time he went to the House of Daena, he somehow always ran into Kaveh. Amid the sea of dark-uniformed students, the ever-energetic Kaveh always stood out like a bright splash of color. Watching Kaveh's always-buoyant, always-proud back, Alhaitham thought he truly deserved that title — the Light of the Kshahrewar. And yet this lively senior never seemed to notice him at all. Which was fine, Alhaitham thought — he wasn't someone who liked stirring up trouble. As long as he could still see him, that was enough. As two of the Akademiya's most gifted students, one from the Haravatat and one from the Kshahrewar, both Kaveh and Alhaitham had been crowned "geniuses" by the school. Genius is lonely — but there's a difference between lonely and alone. At least until that reunion later on, Alhaitham always felt that although Kaveh was lonely, he never seemed alone, because that senior, two years above him, always had all kinds of people around him — from underclassmen younger than him to senior professors with decades of standing at the Akademiya. Different as they all were, their opinion of Kaveh was strangely unanimous: warm, eager to learn, a rare genius even among the Kshahrewar.</p><p>Alhaitham liked hiding in some empty corner to secretly watch Kaveh's back. Kaveh seemed to have a particular fondness for the window seats when working on drafts or projects. The unique stained-glass windows of the House of Daena softened the sunlight pouring in from outside, and the corner Kaveh always favored happened to catch the best of it — under that light, absorbed in his drawing, he seemed to shine like daylight itself. Alhaitham would tuck himself against a nearby bookshelf and keep quietly watching him. He found he could read Kaveh's small expressions well enough to guess what he was thinking — a slight furrow of the brow meant self-doubt; a sudden bright flicker in those ruby eyes meant he was pleased with the design in front of him. Watching the young man's shadow lengthen slowly in the daylight, Alhaitham only then became aware of time passing at all.</p><p>So exactly when is a shadow longest? Is it in the morning, when the sun rises — or every evening, at dusk — or is it whenever I'm watching your back.</p><p>That fleeting glimpse of Kaveh in the House of Daena library, back in his youth, was enough to cement the young man's uncommon place in his heart. To Alhaitham, Kaveh's existence felt like a bright mirror; through him, a side of the world he'd never known was slowly opening up before him. Alhaitham never once imagined that Kaveh would actually speak to him.</p><p>"Please, kōhai, I really need your help with this — please work on this project with me!"</p><p>Alhaitham looked at Kaveh, palms pressed together, pleading in front of him. He was used to working alone and had never had any interest in collaborating with others; he knew his own personality well enough — it was precisely because he was too particular, too different, that he'd chosen to keep his distance from groups in the first place. But the person standing in front of him was Kaveh. Alhaitham had meant to refuse, but his body turned out to be far more honest than he'd expected. Before he knew it, a single word — "Fine" — had already left his mouth.</p><p>"Really?! That's wonderful — our first group meeting is tomorrow morning at nine, don't be late, alright, Haitham?" Kaveh, thrilled, grabbed Alhaitham's hand in thanks, and Alhaitham saw clearly, reflected in those excited ruby eyes, his own faintly flustered self. Kaveh waved goodbye to him and left; Alhaitham stood rooted to the spot for a long moment. It took him a while to come back to himself — what was he even doing, right, the person he'd been quietly watching all this time had finally noticed him too. In an instant, some strange feeling took root in his chest. Kaveh's warmth still seemed to linger on his hand; for once, the ever-rational Alhaitham found himself genuinely dazed. What exactly was this feeling?</p><p>The collision of two geniuses ended, in the end, with a torn-up paper. Alhaitham never once felt his own approach had been at fault, but watching Kaveh, furious, tear apart the thesis they'd co-authored right in front of him, he still felt something crack, clearly, somewhere inside himself.</p><p>Although a difference of opinion over the project had left them on bad terms during their student years, and Alhaitham had once assumed they'd parted ways for good and would never see each other again, even after years apart, he — a man who prided himself on reason and composure — would still, every so often, find himself thinking of that graceful golden silhouette. His daylight. Alhaitham had always assumed that with his reputation as the Light of the Kshahrewar and his considerable talent, Kaveh's life surely couldn't be going too badly — but that reunion at the tavern showed him a very different side of Kaveh. Kaveh's acting was clumsy enough to fool everyone else, but it never once fooled Alhaitham. That night, for the first time, he broke his own usual habits and listened all the way through every one of Kaveh's troubles — and by the end of it, Alhaitham had made the most reckless decision of his twenty-some years of life: he decided to give Kaveh a home.</p><p>Afterward, Kaveh moved in with him without much resistance, but Alhaitham could clearly feel that the feelings that had taken root back in their student years were being amplified, little by little. Though on the surface he remained as cold and unreadable as ever, the emotion underneath had long since become impossible to suppress. Alhaitham felt like a long-dormant volcano — he had no idea when it might suddenly erupt.</p><p>Fortunately, the blond young man behind him, lost in his sketching, hadn't noticed the intensity of the gaze Alhaitham was fixing on him. Just as well, Alhaitham thought. He'd already worked out most of the text on the slab — it recorded the story behind the very statue in this room. He allowed himself a small smile; even though his rational side had no particular interest in love stories like this, that didn't mean Kaveh wouldn't. What kind of reaction would Kaveh have, once he learned the story behind the statue? After thinking it over for a while, he finally spoke, breaking the silence first: "I've finished translating the text on the slab. Kaveh, do you want to hear a story? It's written on the slab — the story hidden behind this statue."</p><p>04</p><p>Andrew and Kurt. These two were the protagonists of the story.</p><p>Even in Khaenri'ah, a nation at the technological peak of its era and relatively open in its thinking, love between two men was still strictly forbidden. Like every nation, Khaenri'ah had once passed through a period of glory, and during that time its rich artistic and academic climate had nurtured a great many painters and architects; artworks had, in turn, become commodities with a market price of their own. Because of that, in pursuit of both reputation and money, artists threw themselves into their creations, and in this era, a flood of exquisite artworks emerged one after another. Among them, the most highly priced sculptures almost always came from the hands of one gifted sculptor: Kurt. But despite being a gifted sculptor, Kurt had lost both his parents while still young, and he had no other family left. What he went through in childhood left him increasingly closed off — quiet, gloomy, a cold air about him that seemed to push away anyone who tried to get close.</p><p>On a rainy day, Kurt, huddled in a street corner, finally met the first person in his life who ever reached out a hand to him — the sculptor Arnold. Arnold was a well-known sculptor in Khaenri'ah, gentle by nature, though for reasons of his own he'd never started a family. Arnold was a kind man; he took in the homeless Kurt, patiently taught him how to select the right stone for carving, how to sculpt a figure's expression — and, most important of all, gave him something like a warm harbor. Thanks to Arnold's patient guidance and Kurt's own natural gift, it wasn't long before Kurt began to make a name for himself among Khaenri'ah's sculptors.</p><p>But heaven showed no mercy to this ill-fated child. On an otherwise ordinary afternoon, Kurt received terrible news — his most beloved teacher, Arnold, had lost his footing while out in the mountains selecting stone, fallen off a cliff, and died on the spot. Learning of his teacher's death, Kurt, already somewhat closed off by nature, grew even more withdrawn. He shut himself away, refusing to leave his room, practicing the carving techniques his teacher had taught him without pause, day or night.</p><p>If he'd never met Andrew, Kurt probably would have spent the rest of his life exactly like this — unremarkably.</p><p>The day he met Andrew was a clear, cloudless afternoon. Kurt hadn't originally planned to go out, but his carving knife had broken, and after searching the whole house for a spare blade and finding nothing, he had no choice but to throw on a coat and head to the nearest art-supply market. Just as Kurt finished buying the carving knife he needed and was heading home, he saw Andrew. Deep blue eyes, an androgynous profile, a head of golden curls — Kurt froze for a moment. A profile that perfect looked like it belonged on a sculptor's stand.</p><p>"Thank you, big sister!" A little girl waved happily at Andrew before running off, excited, toward her mother.</p><p>"Little one, it's big brother, not big sister." Andrew rubbed his forehead, a little helpless — it wasn't the first time he'd been mistaken for a woman. Noticing someone's gaze on him, Andrew turned slowly and caught sight of Kurt, standing there a little at a loss. He walked over easily, smiling, and greeted him. "Hi, I'm Andrew. I'm a knight-in-training with the Knights."</p><p>"Hi, I'm Kurt."</p><p>"Ah, I noticed you kept looking over here — did something happen?"</p><p>"No, nothing."</p><p>"Really?" Andrew's deep blue eyes kept smiling. "We look about the same age — want to be friends?"</p><p>Kurt didn't refuse Andrew — or rather, he didn't really know how to refuse anyone in the first place. From then on, Kurt's gray life took on new color, because of Andrew. Andrew was a chatterbox; every time he saw Kurt he'd babble on about all kinds of stories from the Knights, while Kurt would listen with a smile, pencil in hand, sketching out drafts for whatever model he planned to carve next.</p><p>Until, one day, Andrew brought Kurt to a small park beside the Knights' headquarters, and then went quiet, saying nothing at all. Kurt sensed immediately that something was off with Andrew, but chose to say nothing either — he simply sat beside him and drew, quietly keeping him company. After a long while, Andrew finally spoke.</p><p>"I'm an orphan. The Knights took me in, but the Captain's standards are strict — if I can't pass the trial and become a full knight, I'll have to leave."</p><p>"And, well — as you can see, I didn't pass the trial in the end, so now I have nowhere to go."</p><p>"But it's fine. I can work hard to find a job. Life will get better eventually."</p><p>Watching Andrew's somewhat relieved profile, Kurt couldn't help feeling a pang of sadness. He wanted to say something to comfort him, but what came out instead was something else entirely: "Would you mind coming to live at my place?"</p><p>"Huh?"</p><p>Andrew didn't turn down Kurt's offer, and moved in with him. Living side by side day after day, Kurt came to realize his life could no longer do without Andrew — that somewhere along the way, without even noticing, he'd already fallen for him. Even though Khaenri'ah forbade love between men, Kurt still decided to break through that thin, unspoken wall. To his surprise, after hearing his confession, Andrew didn't refuse him — and just like that, things fell naturally into place, and the two of them became lovers. But only the two of them ever knew it; to everyone else, they were simply seen as very close friends.</p><p>"Hey, Kurt, you're a sculptor, right? So why have you never once sculpted me? You told me before that I'm the most perfect model for a sculpture."</p><p>A pause. Kurt set down his work and turned to look at Andrew. Those clear eyes glimmered, as if waiting expectantly for his answer.</p><p>But Kurt turned away, a little awkward, and said quietly, with his back to Andrew: "There will be one."</p><p>"I'll be looking forward to it."</p><p>Though they were lovers living together, given the times, Kurt and Andrew never did anything more than the mildest intimacy. The only thing they ever really did as a couple was hold hands while walking around town. Because he'd inherited all of his teacher Arnold's skills, Kurt quickly became a sculptor second in fame only to his own master. Andrew, meanwhile, chose to work at a restaurant. Their life together wasn't especially rich, but it was calm and content. Nothing in the world could be happier than having a lover within reach and a stable homeland.</p><p>Unfortunately, that period of glory and peace didn't last long. When the Cataclysm swallowed the entire nation whole, Andrew and Kurt managed, through every means they could find, to escape from underground up to Sumeru above. Watching Sumeru ravaged by the calamity, its people suffering, Andrew felt a deep pang of guilt; once a knight-in-training, he made up his mind to join the Order of the Cygnus, both to honor his own knightly code and to atone, in his own way, for what his homeland had caused. Kurt couldn't stop his stubborn lover, and could only watch, helpless, as Andrew resolutely walked toward the pitch-black battlefield. But lacking in experience, Andrew was killed not long after, fighting against the dark monsters.</p><p>When the news of Andrew's death arrived, Kurt happened to be cleaning the house. The soldier who came to deliver the news found, unexpectedly, that Kurt's face showed no sign of grief at all. After the soldier left, Kurt finally couldn't hold it in any longer. He slid down against the wall, strength gone, tears sliding down his face, staring at the achingly familiar room that no longer carried Andrew's presence, every small moment they'd shared together flashing past in his mind.</p><p>"Hey, Kurt, you're a sculptor, right? So why have you never once sculpted me? You told me before that I'm the most perfect model for a sculpture."</p><p>"I'll be looking forward to it."</p><p>That's right. Andrew had died without ever seeing the sculpture Kurt had promised to make of him. They were lovers, but all these years, it had always been Kurt who was cared for. No matter how hard their life together had been, Andrew had never once shown him a trace of sadness. Kurt understood — Andrew had long since sensed how sensitive he was underneath, and hadn't wanted his own emotions to disturb Kurt's work, so he'd simply carried it all alone, silently, by himself. In the end, Kurt realized, he was the one who owed Andrew.</p><p>Kurt felt his heart shatter into countless pieces, each fragment cutting through him like a small blade, slicing through every nerve, one by one. He strode to the drawer and pulled out the carving knife that had accompanied him through most of his life.</p><p>05</p><p>....</p><p>A silence fell. In the vast stone chamber, all that could be heard was the faint scratch of pencil on paper and the occasional soft rustle of turning pages. Alhaitham still sat with his back to Kaveh, knowing that after a story like that, Kaveh would need some time to process it. When he noticed the sound of drawing behind him had stopped, Alhaitham finally spoke. "So. What is it you want to say this time?"</p><p>"How long were they together?"</p><p>"Ten years, according to the record."</p><p>"Ten years. That's a long time."</p><p>"And yet they still ended up apart, in the end."</p><p>"But for them, having each other, being there for each other — even just ten years is already a long time."</p><p>"For the sculptor who managed to carve out something this exquisite, I genuinely admire him. Whether it was Khaenri'ah, hundreds of years ago, awash in art, or Sumeru now, where art is so tightly restricted — to see a masterpiece like this, in either era, is genuinely no small thing. No one who pursues art walks that path without loneliness; I'm sure his path with his lover was full of hardship too. Time is the cruelest poison for most things — as time passes, people so easily let their original intentions slip away somewhere along the road. And what's most tragic is that most of them never even notice they've already lost what they once thought was the most precious thing they had."</p><p>Kaveh looked down at the sketch he'd nearly finished, then back up at the statue, and felt a sudden bitterness rise in his throat. "Alhaitham, tell me — what is 'love,' exactly? What does 'love' even mean, for a person?"</p><p>"The notebook my mother left me says she thought 'love' was companionship. But I've always felt 'love' isn't quite that simple."</p><p>…</p><p>Another silence.</p><p>Kaveh tilted his head, steadying his own emotions. He silently cursed Alhaitham for being an emotional block of wood, and then tossed out another question he thought might be a little easier to answer: "When I first started learning to paint, my mother taught me that everything in the world has its own color. My father, from the Rtawahist, said something similar — that every star has its own color too. So, Alhaitham, if 'love' had a color, what color do you think it would be?"</p><p>Alhaitham's brow furrowed slightly — Kaveh's question had genuinely stumped him. Rational as he was, he'd never known how to answer this kind of emotional question; on any other day he probably would have just changed the subject, but the seriousness in Kaveh's voice this time made him feel he owed him a real answer. Alhaitham thought it over for a moment. "No emotion is stronger than 'love.' If it had a color, it should be white — a bride's wedding dress is white, and by common reasoning, people think 'love' should be pure and untainted, so no color suits 'love' better than white."</p><p>Kaveh, rare for him, didn't argue back. "That's true. A lot of people think 'love' is black and white — that it has to be sweeping and dramatic. But when I was little, watching my parents love each other, I thought 'love' should be red. My parents never treated me like an outsider — they'd kiss, hold each other, right in front of me, without a second thought. That kind of unguarded warmth is the most sincere thing a person can express. That heat, for a long time, made me feel like nothing suited 'love' better than red. But today, looking at this statue in front of us, I think 'love' is gold — because even though the two of them have long been separated by life and death, the bond between them still shines, bright as sunlight. Even now, no matter how many years have passed, no matter whether anyone remembers them — because they held on, this thing called 'love' can shine like gold, never fading, never tarnishing. Their feelings for each other never dulled with time, even after all these years."</p><p>"Honestly, I envy them a little. They weren't afraid of the world's judgment; they cared about each other; they had a real sense of who they were and what they wanted. Even though they were separated by life and death in the end, that depth of feeling still moves me. Andrew was willing to sacrifice himself for the knightly code he upheld, and in return, Kurt was willing to give up everything just to create the most perfect work his heart could imagine. Right up to their very last moments, they stayed exactly who they'd always been — the sculptor and the knight-in-training. As an idealist myself, I can give everything I have for my ideals, and yet I've never really stopped to ask, or cared, what kind of person I actually am. When I was little, my father, from the Rtawahist, once told me every star has its own color. People are the same. He told me once that in his eyes, my mother was a shining gold, because she was the sun in his world. So, Alhaitham — how would you define me? What color am I, in your eyes?"</p><p>"You're asking the obvious. You're Kaveh — the famous architect Kaveh, the Light of the Kshahrewar, a genius of the Akademiya, the creator of the Palace of Alcazarzaray."</p><p>"But that's just who I am in everyone else's eyes." Kaveh's ruby-red eyes dimmed a little. "I don't even know how to describe myself anymore. As time's gone by, I've had so many labels stuck onto me that I can't even tell what's really me underneath them all."</p><p>Listening to Kaveh, Alhaitham opened his mouth, as if trying to say something, but in the end, he stayed silent. Seeing no response from Alhaitham, Kaveh gave a helpless little laugh — of course, there was no getting anywhere with this block of wood. Days of drawing in a row had left Kaveh exhausted, and now that there happened to be a free pillow right behind him, he leaned back without hesitation. As expected, Alhaitham didn't push him away, and let him lean there. Feeling the familiar warmth and scent coming from behind him, Kaveh, after all these exhausting days, finally felt his body and mind loosen, just a little. Surrounded by that warmth, the headache from the ley line disorder seemed to ease, if only faintly.</p><p>Kaveh picked his pencil back up, sketching idly on the page. "Honestly, I've always wanted to live up to the person the world expects me to be. I've had too many identities all at once — I'm someone's kōhai, I'm the genius everyone talks about, and I'm also my mother's son."</p><p>"My mother went far away, the Palace of Alcazarzaray was destroyed by the domain of the dead — for a good while, those two things caused me a lot of pain. Because of the whole 'genius' and 'Light of the Kshahrewar' thing, I got very good at hiding how fragile I actually felt underneath — and then that day at the tavern, you saw straight through it in one glance. Alhaitham, what kind of person are you, exactly, that you can read everyone so precisely?"</p><p>"But it's only now that I really understand — whether it was that key you handed back to me at the tavern that day, or that thesis with both our names on it that got torn apart, or every single one of our arguments — I have to admit, even after everything that came between us, I never once actually forgot about you."</p><p>"Ever since I moved into your place, whenever something happens to me — good or bad — your face is always the first thing that flashes through my mind. I don't even know what's wrong with me. I keep thinking I should forget it, and yet I can't stop thinking about it, over and over."</p><p>"But looking at this statue in front of us, Alhaitham, I think I've finally come to understand what my own heart actually feels. I think, somewhere in all this, maybe, possibly, probably, I've fallen for you. I have to admit — you really are the most important person in my life, apart from my parents. My mother left a note in her notebook saying that mutual understanding matters, of course, but what matters most is companionship."</p><p>"So — thank you, Alhaitham, for staying by my side this whole time."</p><p>"A thank-you like that ought to come with three times the usual sincerity. Say it again."</p><p>…</p><p>When the answer he'd been expecting never came, Alhaitham realized the person behind him had suddenly gone still. The pencil in Kaveh's hand slipped and rolled to the floor. Alhaitham realized — Kaveh had fallen asleep. Of course he had. This lofty idealist, this senior of his — whenever he found something he truly loved, he'd throw himself at it without a second thought. That was Kaveh. Or maybe, that was simply what Kaveh was meant to be.</p><p>Actually, Alhaitham hadn't come to the Ruin Serpent's for official business at all. This time, Kaveh, out of anger, hadn't told him his plans in advance; Alhaitham had initially assumed Kaveh had just wandered off casually to some familiar spot in the desert. But nearly a full week with no word from Kaveh at all had left him more unsettled than he'd ever felt before. He'd distracted Cyno with the back of a playing card just to pry the truth about Hadramaveth out of Tighnari. Alhaitham had long known that a nation that fell five hundred years ago lay buried beneath Sumeru's desert, but he'd never imagined Kaveh would actually go somewhere that dangerous, chasing something as vague as inspiration. If something had happened to him — Alhaitham didn't dare let himself finish that thought.</p><p>By the time he came back to himself, he realized he was already standing in front of the gate to Khaenri'ah, right beside the hidden chamber Kaveh had happened to discover. He'd taken the stairs of that chamber two at a time and finally found, at the end of it, that familiar gold-and-red silhouette in the stone room. The moment Kaveh turned to face him, Alhaitham felt the tension that had gripped him for nearly a week finally, finally let go.</p><p>Alhaitham turned slowly. His bird of paradise had already fallen fast asleep, the tips of his golden hair brushing lightly against his neck, an itch he barely registered. Feeling the warm breath against his shoulder, Alhaitham used one hand to remove his own cloak and drape it over Kaveh's shoulders. He pulled him a little closer into his arms, and Kaveh, as if sensing it, nuzzled gently against his chest. Looking at the dark circles under Kaveh's eyes, Alhaitham concluded he must not have slept much lately. With that thought, he wrapped one arm around Kaveh, carefully adjusting his position so he could rest a little more comfortably.</p><p>He slowly picked up the pencil off the floor, and happened to catch a glimpse of the sketchbook still in Kaveh's hand. It was indeed a drawing of the statue — except now, the two figures locked in that kiss on the page had become the two of them instead. Well — turns out being on the same wavelength matters, after all.</p><p>Alhaitham smiled quietly to himself. It was the first time all week he'd felt the tightness in his chest finally ease. He raised a hand and summoned Mehrak, packing Kaveh's drawing tools and sketchbook away together. Just as he finished, the first ray of morning light broke through the gap in the coffered ceiling above, warm daylight spilling across Kaveh's familiar sleeping face — just like the very first time he'd ever seen him. The same person, the same familiar face — except this time, the once-untouchable senior who used to feel so far out of reach was now resting, asleep, in his own arms.</p><p>The little bird in his arms stirred. His long lashes fluttered; Kaveh's brow furrowed slightly, and he burrowed his head a little deeper, as if trying to escape the glare of the light. Seeing this, Alhaitham turned his body slightly to shield Kaveh from the worst of the sun. The person in his arms seemed satisfied with the gesture, lips moving faintly, murmuring something too soft to catch at first.</p><p>Alhaitham leaned his ear closer and caught it clearly: Haitham. Haitham — that was what Kaveh used to call him, back before they'd had their falling-out. He hadn't heard that name from Kaveh's mouth in a long, long time. Hearing it again now, Alhaitham felt something between surprise and quiet shock.</p><p>Something surged in his chest again. Looking at the defenseless, sleeping face in his arms, at those full lips, Alhaitham felt the urge to kiss him rise up — but just as he actually lowered his head to do it, he stopped himself, for reasons he couldn't quite name.</p><p>It was in that exact moment that Alhaitham finally, fully understood — that feeling from their student days, the one that used to feel like it might burst out of his chest, had a name. It was called "a flutter of the heart," and it was also called "liking someone."</p><p>Only now, belatedly, did he realize that what he felt for Kaveh had never been just liking. It was something deeper — "love." "Liking" could be reckless, unrestrained. But "love" — love holds itself back.</p><p>After a long moment of thought, Alhaitham finally left a kiss on Kaveh's temple. It was so light, it felt like a feather settling on the surface of a lake without so much as a ripple.</p><p>"Kaveh — about that question you asked. All I want to say is, you don't have to worry so much about any of it. You just need to be yourself."</p><p>"After all — you are what you love."</p></div>
<div class="ep-panel" data-ep="2"><p>For the graduating class of 2025.</p><p>Prologue:</p><p>Graduation was never a ceremony. It was never something you're supposed to be congratulated for.</p><p>It's more like — one evening, you take off your gown, walk out into the wind, and suddenly realize this year of your life is over, and you still haven't figured out how to begin the next one.</p><p>Everyone's cheering "congratulations." Only you know that the ending everyone treats as a given isn't really an ending at all — it's just a signal that you have to go somewhere next.</p><p>Episode 1</p><p>The graduation ceremony always breaks up faster than you'd expect.</p><p>The evening wind scattered the ribbons into disarray; the banner was still hanging in front of the admin building, its bright-red "CONGRATULATIONS" sliced into pieces by the sunset, like wrapping paper someone had torn open in a hurry and left where it fell. Scraps of confetti still littered the lawn, trampled flat, catching faint points of gold light all the same.</p><p>Kaveh still hadn't taken off his gown; his tassel swayed faintly in the wind. The bouquet in his hand had been jostled more than once, one rose petal already crushed, as if it had quietly let out a breath of air. He stood off to the side of the crowd, not joining the cluster of people crowding together for photos, just politely hugging a few classmates and fielding a few of the standard questions about what he planned to do next.</p><p>"Is the studio still running? So are you heading back to the city after this?"</p><p>"...I'll see."</p><p>He smiled easily, like he'd already rehearsed this exact kind of breeziness. No one noticed the faint hollowness hidden in that smile.</p><p>The crowd was slowly drifting toward the school gates — some hailing cabs, some being picked up by parents, a group of others excitedly making plans to celebrate downtown that night. Kaveh gripped his bouquet, his fingers going a little pale; his cap was tucked carelessly under his arm, its black tassel sliding down to hang against his sleeve.</p><p>Just as he was heading toward the bus stop —</p><p>Someone pulled up nearby and stopped the car.</p><p>The familiar shape of it caught the low, orange light of sunset — understated, easy to miss, and yet strangely conspicuous, appearing right at this exact moment. As the window rolled down, the wind carried the dusk in with it.</p><p>Alhaitham had one hand resting on the wheel. He glanced over at him.</p><p>"Done?"</p><p>Kaveh froze for a second, then lifted the bouquet in his hand slightly, as if that were answer enough.</p><p>He didn't ask "why are you here," didn't show the kind of bright, obvious surprise you'd expect. He just walked over, circled around the front of the car, stood by the passenger door for a second — as if confirming something — and then pulled the door open.</p><p>Alhaitham glanced at him. "Get in. That gown's a little too eye-catching."</p><p>"...Wait, let me get my cap."</p><p>He set the cap — the symbol of everything called "graduation" — on his lap, the motion gentle, as if he were setting down something fragile.</p><p>As the car started, the recording from the ceremony was still looping in the background — "Congratulations to the Class of—" — the sound fading further and further into the background as they pulled away. The sun was about to sink. As the car moved, the light slid, inch by inch, off Kaveh's shoulder.</p><p>The steering wheel turned slightly, but the car wasn't heading downtown.</p><p>That road led to the park.</p><p>Kaveh turned his head, pressing a hand against the bouquet. "...You know there are still people out there celebrating, right?"</p><p>"There's more than one way to celebrate."</p><p>Alhaitham didn't look at him, his tone even. "You're better suited to something quieter."</p><p>Kaveh said nothing, just looked down at the cap in his lap.</p><p>He suddenly realized — a year had ended.</p><p>But that sense of ending wasn't a celebration. It felt more like — standing at a fork in the road, wind passing by his ears, and yet unable to make out any direction in it.</p><p>Episode 2</p><p>The park wasn't far from campus, but the road on graduation day felt longer than usual.</p><p>The sun had already sunk completely, leaving only a thin gold line at the edge of the sky, like a scratch someone had left there by accident with a fingernail. The trees along the street swayed gently in the wind, their leaves dyed a deep wine color, making a soft sound underfoot when stepped on — not a crisp crunch, but something slower, more muffled.</p><p>Alhaitham didn't park in the lot; instead he pulled up on the side closer to the riverbank. He switched off the headlights as he stepped out, and in that instant the light disappeared entirely, leaving only the color of the sky and the distant flow of traffic on the highway.</p><p>Kaveh got out holding his bouquet, cap tucked under his arm, his movements a little stiff.</p><p>There weren't many people in the park — occasionally a graduate or two would pass by on the other side holding balloons, taking photos, scattered laughter drifting over across the grass. The two of them walked along the riverside path, footsteps thinning out into the wind as they stepped over the stone tiles.</p><p>They weren't walking side by side — not out of distance, exactly, but more like — the kind of important conversation that never quite happens when you're standing too close together.</p><p>After a few steps, Alhaitham spoke, direct as always:</p><p>"You're not going back to the city?"</p><p>Kaveh lowered his gaze, his fingers absently rubbing at the wrapping paper on the bouquet, making a faint rustling sound.</p><p>"...How did you know?"</p><p>"You didn't mention the studio at the ceremony."</p><p>The wind blew past, carrying the smell of the river, carrying away the silence with it for a moment.</p><p>Kaveh gave a small laugh, not so much covering something up as mocking himself a little. "I thought you'd say something like, 'you haven't even packed your bags yet.'"</p><p>"Luggage is just a state," Alhaitham said flatly. "It's the way you look at things that's different."</p><p>Kaveh looked up, a little startled. "...You really pay close attention, don't you."</p><p>Alhaitham didn't answer, just kept walking a couple of steps ahead, waiting for him to catch up.</p><p>The streetlamps lit up one by one beside them, casting pools of light onto the road like a broken, intermittent guideline. Kaveh passed through the light, the edge of his gown catching a brief flash of brightness before slipping back into shadow.</p><p>"I want to stay," he finally said, not too fast. "Not the kind of staying where I become a teacher here — I know that's what you'd assume."</p><p>Alhaitham turned to look at him, his eyes waiting for the rest of the sentence.</p><p>"I just..." Kaveh paused, as if searching for a word that could accurately describe what he was feeling. "This past year, living here, I watched so many architecture students working on their projects — their budgets never enough, their equipment always outdated, even the models they took to competitions, they had to scrape together the money for themselves."</p><p>He paused, his voice dropping lower.</p><p>"I suddenly felt like — my designs shouldn't just go running off to make money in commercial projects."</p><p>The moment those words landed, the wind happened to stop for a few seconds, and the air seemed to hold some faint tremor in it.</p><p>Alhaitham didn't respond right away, just stayed quiet in that silence, his gaze steady.</p><p>"...You're sure."</p><p>Not opposition, not agreement either — just confirmation.</p><p>Kaveh tightened his grip on the bouquet; there was light in his eyes, but not the eager kind that comes with naive idealism — something steadier, deeper, the feeling of "I know this road won't be easy, but I want to try anyway."</p><p>He turned his head, looking at the river's surface rippled by the evening wind, and said quietly:</p><p>"I want to make something for this place. Even if it's just a little something, that's fine too."</p><p>In that moment, he wasn't looking at Alhaitham.</p><p>But when the wind blew, a voice came from beside him, so quiet it was barely there:</p><p>"— Then stay."</p><p>No surprise, no lengthy objection. His tone was like stating a simple fact, the same way he'd said, "Get in, that gown's a little too eye-catching."</p><p>As if — to Alhaitham, the world had plenty of places to go, but wherever Kaveh wanted to go, as long as it wasn't running away, was fine by him. From the moment he'd first known him, Alhaitham had understood he could never interfere with this person's choices — so, once again, as always, he chose to respect it.</p><p>Episode 3</p><p>By the time they reached the end of the riverside path, the sky had gone completely dark. The water caught the streetlights on its surface, like a mirror someone had cracked, fragments of light shifting in the ripples.</p><p>It was quieter here than near the entrance — even the graduates who'd passed by earlier were gone now, leaving only the distant sound of music, blurred, like the noise of some other city carried across the water.</p><p>Kaveh set the bouquet down beside the bench, his cap resting on his knees, the tassel hanging down, drifting faintly in the wind.</p><p>"...Actually," he began, his voice not as steady as before, "I know finding a job right now is hard."</p><p>Alhaitham didn't interrupt, just turned his gaze toward him, giving him room to keep talking.</p><p>"Not the kind of hard where you send out two résumés and hear nothing back." Kaveh rested his chin on one hand, fingers tracing along the edge of his cap. "It's — the whole industry is leaning more and more toward automation. A lot of companies are already using AI to generate the first draft, and the designer's just there to polish it up afterward. The client thinks it's efficient. The boss thinks it saves money."</p><p>He gave a short laugh, quiet, but you could hear in it not complaint but a kind of tired clarity — "I've seen it for what it is."</p><p>"We used to say design had its own 'personal style.'" He kept going. "Now what people talk about is 'parameter templates.' Even at competitions, the judges are starting to lean toward whoever integrates the smart systems most seamlessly... even a word like 'good-looking' has to get quantified into some data module."</p><p>Alhaitham watched his profile, and spoke slowly. "So that's why you don't want to go back to the city."</p><p>"Mm." Kaveh didn't deny it. "The city has more opportunities, but — the big firms there are all transitioning. If I went back, chances are I'd end up on some intelligent-design team, running variable simulations against parameter sheets all day."</p><p>He paused here, picked up a fallen maple leaf from the ground, looked at it for a second, then let it drop back down.</p><p>"...But I don't want to become the kind of designer where, as long as the data checks out, the drawing goes out the door."</p><p>His voice wasn't loud, but there was a restrained clarity to it. "I know it sounds risky, even a little out of step with the times. But I'm really not built for — staying the same."</p><p>Alhaitham leaned back against the bench, his gaze settling on the gown Kaveh was still wearing, his tone flat, almost without a ripple:</p><p>"You're afraid of staying the same. But you're also afraid of having nothing to fall back on."</p><p>Kaveh froze, his fingers stopping mid-air.</p><p>The wind blew in off the river, cold, stirring the gown on his shoulders, as if reminding him — this outfit, this symbol of "having finished something," was really just a temporary resting point.</p><p>He didn't rush to argue, didn't try to change the subject either, just let out a slow breath.</p><p>"...You're right."</p><p>The river gradually disappeared into the night, the line between water and sky blurring together, leaving only the distant bridge lights, like a signal swaying in the wind.</p><p>Kaveh nudged the maple leaf away with the tip of his shoe, a faint scrape against the stone tile. He wasn't looking at Alhaitham, just staring ahead at the streetlit path, his voice sounding like it was being pushed slowly up from somewhere deep in his chest.</p><p>"...What if I really do stay here for good?"</p><p>No lead-up, no gentle segue — he just threw it out, abrupt. Like tossing a stone into the dark, hearing no splash, but knowing it had really been thrown.</p><p>"This city isn't big. It's not special either." He kept going, his tone not pessimistic, just very matter-of-fact self-assessment. "No major projects, no famous architecture firms. Once graduation season passes, most people leave. A few years from now, this place might look exactly the same — maybe even quieter."</p><p>He paused for a moment, as if checking that the thought hadn't hurt as much as he'd expected, then added, slowly:</p><p>"...Don't you think I'd be standing still?"</p><p>Alhaitham didn't answer right away. He kept looking straight ahead for a long moment, as if weighing what Kaveh's question was really trying to reach.</p><p>Then, what he asked wasn't "so what do we do," and it wasn't "what's your plan going forward" — it was:</p><p>"Would you keep moving, here?"</p><p>Kaveh turned his head, not quite following. "...What?"</p><p>Alhaitham looked at him, his tone still even:</p><p>"A city can stay still. A person can't."</p><p>When those words landed, even the wind seemed to pause for half a second.</p><p>"You staying here—" his voice dropped lower, "—isn't about standing in place."</p><p>Not a challenge, and not encouragement either.</p><p>Kaveh froze. In that instant, he realized — Alhaitham had never, from start to finish, read "staying in a small city" as retreat.</p><p>Silence drifted between them for a while. Kaveh let out a slow breath, the corner of his mouth lifting in a barely visible smile, as if he'd finally confirmed something.</p><p>"...I won't stand still."</p><p>As he said it, the wind picked up again, shaking the shadows beneath the streetlamp.</p><p>Alhaitham lowered his gaze, as if he'd heard everything Kaveh wanted to say, his tone still level:</p><p>"Then that's enough."</p><p>The wind shifted just then, carrying the chill that settles in after the sunset burns off. The river's mist clung to the air like an invisible layer of fog, slowing down their breathing.</p><p>Kaveh didn't hurry to stand and keep walking; instead he sat down slowly on the bench. The wooden seat was cool from the wind. He set his cap down beside him, the brim brushing lightly against the seat, making a faint, thin sound.</p><p>"...But sometimes, I still get scared."</p><p>He wasn't looking at anyone, his gaze resting on the lit stretch of water across the river, as if talking to himself.</p><p>"Scared that one day I'll realize — staying isn't because I actually want to do something here. It's because — I don't dare set out again."</p><p>When he said the word "scared," his voice didn't break, but there was a fragility to it, not easy to catch, seeping out between the words.</p><p>Alhaitham didn't respond right away.</p><p>He moved closer, very slowly, walked around to the other side of the bench, and sat down — not too close, just close enough that when the wind blew from his direction, it overlapped with Kaveh's breathing.</p><p>Between them was the exact width of a graduation cap.</p><p>Silence settled into the night, without pressure, but present all the same.</p><p>Kaveh lowered his eyes, breathing out softly, as if pressing down, for the moment, on feelings he hadn't yet had time to sort through.</p><p>Then —</p><p>A gust of wind blew past, and Kaveh's shoulder swayed slightly under it. He didn't pull back, just leaned naturally with the motion, his shoulder drawing an inch closer to Alhaitham's side.</p><p>It wasn't a deliberate move closer — more like he was checking something: whether it was alright to rest here, even just for a moment.</p><p>As that inch of distance closed, Alhaitham tilted his head, his gaze calm and clear, as if quietly weighing this small gesture.</p><p>He didn't say "don't be scared."</p><p>He simply raised a hand and tapped, lightly, once, on the wooden slat of the bench right behind Kaveh's shoulder.</p><p>"No one ever really knows where the next step is going."</p><p>His tone was even, without any note of comfort in it, almost cold, even.</p><p>"But the fact that you're thinking about it — that's already enough."</p><p>Kaveh's hand still rested on his lap, his knuckles a little pale from gripping the bouquet for so long. He slowly loosened his grip, his fingers sliding down along the fabric, finally coming to rest at the edge of the bench, just a few centimeters from Alhaitham's side.</p><p>The streetlight cast two shadows at their feet, crossing over each other; the wind moved across the water, carrying a sound almost too faint to hear — like the night, exhaling.</p><p>Kaveh watched those overlapping shadows, his voice dropping low, as if afraid of disturbing something:</p><p>"...You already knew I wasn't going back to the city, didn't you?"</p><p>It landed like a word dropped out of the wind — weightless, and yet impossible to ignore.</p><p>Alhaitham didn't answer right away. He simply shifted his gaze away from the river, letting it settle on the hand Kaveh had left resting at the edge of the bench.</p><p>The wind blew again just then, and that small distance between them seemed to shrink a little further, as if pushed by it.</p><p>"You've never been the kind of person—" his voice wasn't loud, but it was completely certain, leaving no room for doubt, "—who walks back the way they came."</p><p>Kaveh froze — not because the words caught him off guard, but because, in that instant, he suddenly understood: all his back-and-forth hesitation, in this person's eyes, had never been "being lost." It had always been evidence of "still moving forward."</p><p>He didn't answer, just laughed softly, the sound carrying a kind of release that comes after exhaustion.</p><p>His hand didn't fully reach over — just his fingertips brushed lightly against the far end of the bench, as if one more inch would be enough for them to touch.</p><p>Alhaitham didn't take his hand, and he didn't pull away either. He just slowed his breathing, letting that small closeness exist there, reasonably, in the dark.</p><p>That was the kind of closeness that comes with growing up — not possession, not a declaration either, but simply: I'm right here, waiting for you to decide whether you want to close the distance.</p><p>Episode 4</p><p>Night had fully settled now; the riverside path in the park had grown quiet. Only the light on the water traced out a thin, bright line, like a pause someone had deliberately left in place.</p><p>Kaveh was still holding that same posture — fingertips resting at the edge of the bench, as if he might really lean over at any moment, or as if he were simply pausing there, watching which way the wind would go.</p><p>Just then, Alhaitham moved.</p><p>He didn't take Kaveh's hand, and he didn't suddenly stand up either — he just reached one hand into his coat pocket and, from a place that seemed entirely wrong for it, pulled out a bottle of banana milk in slightly conspicuous packaging.</p><p>He held it up in front of Kaveh, gave it a small shake, and said flatly: "Here."</p><p>Kaveh blinked, taking two seconds to register what it was, then his eyes went wide. "...Where did you even get this?"</p><p>"Convenience store," Alhaitham said, his tone as even as if he'd been asked whether the water was boiling yet.</p><p>Kaveh took the bottle, his fingers meeting the cold of the packaging, and it seemed to pull him, just a little, out of whatever he'd been feeling. He glanced down at the label, disbelieving.</p><p>"I — I spent half an hour yesterday searching that Asian grocery store in this little town! I even checked the second shelf from the top! And you're telling me — the convenience store just has it?"</p><p>Alhaitham glanced at him, his tone almost offhand: "Did you look down?"</p><p>Kaveh froze. "...The shelf's that low? Of course I—"</p><p>He didn't get to finish the sentence before something clicked, and his expression turned faintly complicated.</p><p>Alhaitham leaned back against the bench, his gaze on him, and pointed out, unhurried: "You always check the top row and the front row. You never really look at the middle."</p><p>"...Who checks that spot on purpose!" Kaveh caught up with himself and glared at him. "And besides, banana milk usually goes on the—"</p><p>"You didn't fail to find it," Alhaitham said flatly. "You just didn't bend down."</p><p>The moment those words landed, Kaveh froze for a beat.</p><p>Not because he'd been teased — but because he suddenly realized that sentence didn't seem to be only about the banana milk.</p><p>The wind lifted the edge of the gown still on his shoulders; the bottle in his hand still carried the chill of having just come out of a fridge.</p><p>Kaveh looked at the bottle of banana milk and let out a very soft laugh — not the laugh of being amused, but the kind that comes after being seen through completely, and simply accepting it.</p><p>"...Fine." He tore open the straw and stuck it in. "Thanks."</p><p>Alhaitham didn't respond, just leaned back and let his gaze drift away, out toward the now-quiet water.</p><p>Kaveh took a sip; the sweetness burst in his mouth, mixed with the cool night air, giving him the strange, faint illusion of almost wanting to cry. Not from sadness — but from suddenly realizing, in this exact moment, that even a place where you'd stopped could still hold a little warmth.</p><p>He turned his head, as if without meaning to, lifted the bottle and gave it a small shake, his voice dropping low:</p><p>"...How did you even know I'd want this?"</p><p>Alhaitham didn't look at him, just said, plainly:</p><p>"On your shopping list, you circled the exclamation point next to 'banana milk' three times."</p><p>Kaveh froze, then said, very quietly: "...You actually read that kind of thing?"</p><p>"Happened to notice it," he said, his tone unchanged as ever.</p><p>But it was exactly that "happened to notice it" that felt, in the dark, like a very small but unmistakably visible light switching on.</p><p>"...Sometimes I really don't feel like an adult." Kaveh lowered his head, staring at the stone tiles under his feet, his voice quiet, as if afraid of disturbing the night. "Like I'm still in high school, unsettled, my heart suddenly going hollow for no reason."</p><p>Alhaitham said nothing, just kept walking forward along the path.</p><p>The streetlamps lit up one after another, casting long, thin shadows at their feet.</p><p>"Adults lose things too," he said flatly.</p><p>Kaveh went still for a second, then let the corner of his mouth curve up. He wasn't sure if it was because of the words, or because the wind had simply cleared his head.</p><p>They stood under the streetlamp for a few seconds together; the wind mussed the loose strands of hair on Kaveh's forehead, and he didn't bother fixing them, just looked out at the water and, quietly, out of nowhere, said:</p><p>"...Being loved really does feel good."</p><p>He wasn't saying it to anyone in particular — more like saying it to the wind.</p><p>"I used to think that once my mother remarried, it meant she didn't want me anymore."</p><p>He said it without any catch in his voice, without any deliberate restraint either — just a plain, quiet calm.</p><p>"Even now, I don't fully believe she 'doesn't love me.' I just — I've never been able to confirm whether the way she loves still has room for me in it."</p><p>The wind blew past, and he lowered his eyes.</p><p>"So sometimes I wonder if maybe I'm just not good at — being someone people keep in mind."</p><p>Alhaitham didn't respond.</p><p>He just stood there quietly beside Kaveh; when the wind blew past, he stood steady, like an anchor the night couldn't push aside.</p><p>Kaveh didn't wait for an answer, and didn't seem to need an explanation either. He just took a light breath, and then —</p><p>"Let's go."</p><p>He walked ahead on his own, as if he'd tucked that sentence — "being loved really does feel good" — into the wind, and had no intention of looking back at it.</p><p>Episode 5</p><p>By the time they got back to the car, the night had grown deep. Their reflections showed faintly on the window glass, the black of their gowns blending into the dark. Kaveh opened the door, carefully set the bouquet down in the back seat, then climbed into the passenger side and started, naturally, undoing the buttons of his gown.</p><p>"Changing clothes in your car — that's not against some rule, is it?" He said it with a note of teasing finally back in his voice.</p><p>"Windows are tinted." Alhaitham started the engine, his tone flat. "It's not the first time you've changed in this car."</p><p>"...You realize saying it like that just makes people misunderstand, right?"</p><p>Kaveh muttered his complaint under his breath while folding his gown and pulling his own jacket back on. He glanced at himself in the rearview mirror, straightening his hair a little; he looked far more relaxed now than he had right after leaving the ceremony.</p><p>The car pulled onto the main road, streetlights sliding past the window one after another. Kaveh leaned back in his seat, turning to look at him. "Where are you taking me?"</p><p>"That Chinese restaurant you mentioned before, the one you waited an hour in line for and never got a table," Alhaitham said flatly.</p><p>"...You remember even that?"</p><p>"You sent me three voice messages complaining about how long the line was."</p><p>"And you can say it this calmly—"</p><p>Before he could finish, the car turned onto a small street lit warm and yellow, the neon signs reflecting off the windshield like a burst of sudden fireworks.</p><p>The night air of the small town carried the smell of food, the scattered noise of a crowd, and a kind of lightness that felt like "not a grand ending, just life continuing to move forward."</p><p>The restaurant sat on a quiet street in the middle of town, its dim yellow light falling over the damp pavement, carrying a plain, genuine kind of warmth.</p><p>When the car stopped, a few locals were still walking home carrying plastic bags; the smell of the evening's cooking oil, chili, and soy sauce drifted out through the door's gap. Kaveh took a breath of it and laughed.</p><p>"— After a graduation ceremony full of cake everyone's already sick of, I can finally eat something normal, with actual oil and salt and soy sauce in it."</p><p>As he said this, he tossed his gown into a heap in the back seat, and for the first time, his whole body seemed to slip free of some role he'd been expected to play.</p><p>Walking into the restaurant, the light was warm and a little dim; the tabletops were polished stainless steel, and the chair legs made a familiar scraping sound against the floor when dragged — like real, everyday life settling back into place.</p><p>Alhaitham went to grab a number tag while Kaveh stood by the counter, hugging the half-finished bottle of banana milk, looking every bit like a student who'd just escaped the graduation ceremony.</p><p>"What number?" Kaveh leaned over to look.</p><p>"Seventeen." Alhaitham set the tag on the table, then reached over and tore open a set of chopsticks, handing them to him.</p><p>Kaveh took them, the motion practiced, but he still couldn't help muttering, "You don't have to open these for me every time, I'm not—"</p><p>He stopped mid-sentence, watching Alhaitham tear open a packet of dipping sauce, and suddenly realized that this kind of casual, being-taken-care-of everyday-ness was, in itself, a form of intimacy. He didn't finish the thought out loud, just lowered his head to unwrap his own chopsticks, letting the conversation drift somewhere else instead:</p><p>"You know, AI can even generate menu designs on its own now — even the 'popular color palette' gets calculated by big data these days."</p><p>As he talked, he spun the chopsticks between his fingers, as if trying to shake off the last of the ceremony's atmosphere entirely.</p><p>"Technology was invented to assist people." His gaze landed on the menu screen above the counter, the images cycling through it too smooth, too flawless, too pretty, and completely lacking any warmth.</p><p>"That's how it's supposed to be, anyway."</p><p>Alhaitham leaned back against his chair, listening quietly as he finished the thought, not interrupting.</p><p>Kaveh paused for a moment, looking up at those standardized, near-perfect food photos, as if asking himself the question, or maybe asking it of the era itself:</p><p>"But what we call 'good-looking' these days has to fit into a parameter template."</p><p>He laughed a little, no edge to it, but unmistakably clear-eyed:</p><p>"...We used to say design had 'personal style.'" He repeated the line slowly, the same one he'd said back by the river, as if testing it once more against the reality of daily life:</p><p>"Now what people talk about is — 'how high is the system recognition score.'"</p><p>He stopped there for a second, as if some contradiction was quietly tightening in his chest:</p><p>"I know AI really has improved efficiency, made design more accessible... technology is supposed to make life better."</p><p>He lowered his head, lining up his chopsticks neatly on the table's edge.</p><p>"But if one day even 'good' gets decided by an algorithm — then what's a designer even responsible for, anymore?"</p><p>From the kitchen came the clang of a spatula against a wok, the faint sizzle of frying, the smell of garlic and ginger hitting hot oil, the background noise of the crowd softening into something warm inside all that steam.</p><p>Before long, the first dish arrived — a sizzling hot pot still steaming, the smell hitting them all at once, its color deeper than anything in the standard-color-value photos, the edge slightly charred, as if the chef had swirled the pan one extra time without thinking about it.</p><p>Kaveh's chopsticks paused mid-motion.</p><p>He looked at the dish and let out a quiet laugh, as if he'd finally confirmed something small:</p><p>"...This color would get flagged in a template as 'inconsistent stir-fry timing.'"</p><p>With that, he took a bite.</p><p>It wasn't food meant to "look presentable" or to be photographed for social media — it was the taste of "someone actually working the heat by hand."</p><p>He chewed for a moment, chopsticks pausing mid-air, his eyes shifting with a small, quiet ease that settled in slowly.</p><p>"— This flavor doesn't taste like something a smart system generated."</p><p>Not a compliment, exactly — more a confirmation that a human hand had been here.</p><p>Alhaitham didn't look at the dish, just answered evenly: "So people haven't fully exited the picture yet."</p><p>Kaveh froze for a second, then kept eating, the tension that had crept into his shoulders while thinking about AI finally, slowly, loosening.</p><p>He didn't go back to the serious topic, instead sighing in a lighter tone, the kind that comes from walking back from a philosophical fork in the road into an ordinary side street:</p><p>"...Turns out not everything has to be perfect. It can just be delicious."</p><p>Alhaitham slid the dipping sauce over to his side, as if summing it up: "A parameter template can't feed you."</p><p>Kaveh's chopsticks paused for a second; that line landed lightly, but it felt like a nail driven firmly into solid ground, pinning down all the drifting thoughts from that day, pulling them back onto a track he could keep walking on.</p><p>He looked up, his eyes faintly misted from the steam:</p><p>"...Right. It can't feed you."</p><p>Then, finally, he laughed — not the "photo-ready" laugh from the ceremony, not the polite social laugh reserved for questions about the future, but a real one, shoulders fully loosened, the kind of laugh that comes from actually being alive in the night of this small town.</p><p>Steam kept rising from the wok, the air thick with the smell of chili oil. It was a human smell — not a "fragrance value" logged in some database, but the specific, real kind of scent that makes your appetite open up when you catch it.</p><p>Kaveh picked up a piece of meat, not rushing to eat it, just tapping the edge of the pot lightly with his chopsticks, as if checking the temperature.</p><p>"...AI was supposed to be here to help." He said this evenly. "But now it's more like, 'either you join the high-efficiency system, or you get left behind.'"</p><p>He looked at the dish in front of him, its colors uneven, and tapped his chopsticks lightly against the table: "Everyone's stopped asking 'why am I designing it this way' and started asking, 'how fast can I get this turned in.'"</p><p>When he said the word "why," his tone noticeably lightened.</p><p>Not anger — more like a kind of conviction, the sense of "I know this sounds idealistic, but I still want to say it."</p><p>Alhaitham propped his chin on one hand, watching him, and instead of countering with some efficiency-first argument, said, flatly:</p><p>"Tools don't decide what a person becomes."</p><p>As he spoke, a server passed by, the oil at the edge of the pan still crackling faintly. The noise of real life pressed extra weight into those words.</p><p>"The moment someone chooses to stop thinking—" his voice dropped, "—that's the moment they've chosen to exit."</p><p>Kaveh's motion paused.</p><p>That sentence seemed to circle through his heart, peeling back all the contradictions he hadn't been able to put into words, revealing the core underneath:</p><p>Technology isn't the enemy. What's truly frightening is someone giving up the desire to leave a trace behind.</p><p>He put the food in his mouth; the moment he bit down, the slightly charred edge of the pan — that extra second the chef had left it on the flame — tasted messier than anything in the standardized photos, but more alive, too.</p><p>"...But what if most people do give up?" He looked up, his tone light. "What about the ones who still want to keep their own style?"</p><p>Alhaitham glanced at him, his answer as simple as it could be:</p><p>"Then it matters even more to leave a trace."</p><p>Kaveh's chopsticks went still.</p><p>Alhaitham kept going, unhurried:</p><p>"Not the kind of thing that can be copied as data — but something the system can't quantify, and yet the second you see it, you know it's yours."</p><p>He didn't say it with any particular intensity, but that line — "the second you see it, you know it's yours" — carried more weight than any declaration of love could.</p><p>Kaveh's throat moved.</p><p>He suddenly understood — that so-called "humans are the core" wasn't about "opposing AI." It was about "even if AI exists, I still have to prove I was here."</p><p>The wind slipped in through the door, carrying a trace of chili oil, sharp enough to sting the nose faintly.</p><p>Kaveh didn't say anything, just exhaled softly, as if he'd finally found a sentence he could follow up with:</p><p>"...Then I need to make something — something that looks like 'the Kaveh you know.'"</p><p>Alhaitham glanced at him, his tone even, almost like confirming an instruction:</p><p>"Then go make it."</p><p>Episode 6</p><p>After dinner, when the restaurant's glass door swung open, the heat and the smell of chili scattered together into the night, and the evening chill rushed in.</p><p>There weren't many lights on the street; the small town's night didn't have the neon and traffic of the city — it was quiet, almost empty, quiet enough that even their footsteps landed clearly on the stone-paved road.</p><p>They didn't rush back to the car, instead wandering forward without discussing it.</p><p>The conversation about AI was still echoing in their minds, but it didn't feel oppressive — it had left behind, instead, a kind of solid, settled feeling, the sense of "my existence has been acknowledged."</p><p>Kaveh tucked his hands into his jacket pockets, his fingers brushing against the straw wrapper he hadn't thrown away yet, and somehow it made this night suddenly feel — not like "graduation day" at all, more like just an ordinary day.</p><p>The wind in the small town blew under the streetlamps, making the light ripple like water.</p><p>At a corner, Kaveh stopped.</p><p>He wasn't looking at Alhaitham, just pulled his hand out of his pocket — it looked a little pale in the night, though his fingertips still carried a trace of warmth from the hot food.</p><p>The wind blew past, and his fingertips brushed lightly against the back of Alhaitham's hand.</p><p>Not holding it — just a touch.</p><p>In that moment, he seemed to be waiting — not waiting for a response, exactly, but waiting to see whether this small thing would be allowed.</p><p>The wind eased off, just slightly.</p><p>Alhaitham looked down, glancing at the hand he'd extended.</p><p>Then, he didn't grip it tightly.</p><p>— Instead, he simply reached his hand over, laid it alongside Kaveh's, and let his fingers slide into place, interlocking.</p><p>The motion was simple, without any extra hesitation — as if he were just "adjusting his stride," or as if this road had always been meant to be walked side by side.</p><p>In the instant the distance between them closed, Kaveh clearly went still, his breath catching, just slightly, out of rhythm.</p><p>He looked down at their intertwined hands, a small light catching on his knuckles under the streetlamp — not dramatic, just the quiet settledness of something finally clicking into its rightful place.</p><p>"You just said—" Alhaitham glanced sideways at him, his tone picking up an unfinished thread, "—that you wanted to leave a trace."</p><p>Kaveh turned his head, his fingers curling in gently, a hint of a smile in his voice: "Mm."</p><p>Alhaitham: "So — is this the first one?"</p><p>Kaveh didn't answer, just held on tighter.</p><p>— The wind blew past, and their two shadows overlapped under the streetlamp, stretching long into the night.</p></div>
</div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="3"><p>More work is posted on AO3, all of it in Chinese — for now, reading the English means translating it yourself:</p><p><a href="https://archiveofourown.org/users/ExcaliburSchlaflied" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AO3 · ExcaliburSchlaflied →</a></p></div></div><p><a href="/en/writing/">← Back to Writing</a></p></article></Base>

<script is:inline>
  document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', () => {
  document.querySelectorAll('.ep-layout').forEach((layout) => {
    const tabs = layout.querySelectorAll('.ep-tab');
    const panels = layout.querySelectorAll('.ep-panel');
    const orderedTabs = [...tabs].sort((a, b) => Number(a.textContent.match(/\d+/)?.[0]) - Number(b.textContent.match(/\d+/)?.[0]));
    const tabContainer = layout.querySelector('.ep-tabs');
    const panelContainer = layout.querySelector('.ep-panels');
    orderedTabs.forEach((tab) => tabContainer.appendChild(tab));
    orderedTabs.forEach((tab) => panelContainer.appendChild(layout.querySelector('.ep-panel[data-ep="' + tab.dataset.ep + '"]')));
    const getStats = (panel) => {
      const text = [...panel.querySelectorAll('p')].map((paragraph) => paragraph.textContent).join(' ');
      const words = (text.match(/\S+/g) || []).length;
      const minutes = Math.max(1, Math.ceil(words / 200));
      return `${words.toLocaleString('en-US')} words \u00b7 about ${minutes} min read`;
    };
    orderedTabs.forEach((tab) => {
      const panel = layout.querySelector('.ep-panel[data-ep="' + tab.dataset.ep + '"]');
      const stats = getStats(panel);
      const panelTitle = document.createElement('h2');
      panelTitle.className = 'ep-title';
      panelTitle.textContent = tab.textContent.trim();
      const panelMeta = document.createElement('div');
      panelMeta.className = 'ep-reading-meta';
      panelMeta.textContent = `${stats} \u00b7 Fan fiction`;
      panel.prepend(panelMeta);
      panel.prepend(panelTitle);
    });
    tabs.forEach((tab) => {
      tab.addEventListener('click', () => {
        tabs.forEach((t) => t.classList.remove('active'));
        panels.forEach((p) => p.classList.remove('active', 'entering'));
        tab.classList.add('active');
        const panel = layout.querySelector('.ep-panel[data-ep="' + tab.dataset.ep + '"]');
        panel.classList.add('active');
        window.requestAnimationFrame(() => panel.classList.add('entering'));
        panels[0].closest('.ep-panels').scrollTop = 0;
      });
    });
  });
});
</script>

<style>
